  ],
  "opening": "In Chapter 6, we saw that applying a reversible circuit \\(U_f\\) to a superposition of inputs evaluates \\(f(x)\\) on every input at once, yet measuring the register reveals only one random pair \\((x, f(x))\\). To extract collective features of \\(f\\), we first need a way to record \\(f(x)\\) in quantum phases rather than in an entangled target bit.",
  "opening_continuation": "We begin with phase kickback, building step by step from reversible black-box oracles to eigenstates of the bit-flip gate and coherent uncomputation. We then examine global properties of Boolean functions and see why phase encoding unlocks interference where bit storage fails, before putting both pieces together in Deutsch\u2019s algorithm.",
  "intro_continuation": "Classically, finding out whether \\(f(0) = f(1)\\) requires evaluating \\(f\\) twice\u2014once on \\(0\\) and once on \\(1\\)\u2014because knowing \\(f(0)\\) alone leaves both possibilities open. Can a quantum computation answer this question using only a single evaluation of \\(U_f\\)?"
}
